issue.body:
If you make a handlebars helper that returns an html string, you need to pass it through `Handlebars.SafeString()`

From their docs: 

```
Handlebars.registerHelper('link', function(text, url) {
  text = Handlebars.Utils.escapeExpression(text);
  url  = Handlebars.Utils.escapeExpression(url);

  var result = '<a href="' + url + '">' + text + '</a>';

  return new Handlebars.SafeString(result);
});
```

Trying this in an helper results in Handlebars not being defined. 

```
module.exports = function()
{
  text = Handlebars.Utils.escapeExpression(text);
  url  = Handlebars.Utils.escapeExpression(url);

  var result = '<a href="' + url + '">' + text + '</a>';

  return new Handlebars.SafeString(result);
}
```

It works if you add a `var Handlebars = require('Handlebars')` for _every_ single helper.
But this also brings in the entire Handlebars compiler to the client (which is what we don't want, right? :P).

Maybe `handlebars-loader` should provide a shim to helpers that provides the Handlebars global with only the necessary util functions?
